    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d312">

                <pb facs="0397" n="383" sameAs="frus:frus1944v06/pg383"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.002/383: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-03-21T16:00:00+07:00">March 21, 1944—4
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received March 21—12:38 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>500. Soong’s chief henchman in China Defence Supplies here stated privately that
                    the “dust storm” in regard to Soong is blowing over. The Director of European
                    Department of Foreign Office says, also privately, that he thinks Soong will
                    continue on with Foreign Office and suggests as good evidence that when Soong
                    reported on China’s foreign affairs at weekly memorial service March 20 he was
                    openly commended for his report by Chiang Kai-shek. Soong has not yet, however,
                    begun to attend or give usual functions, although he has been going to the
                    Foreign Office for short period almost every day.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
